{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Teaching in Tech-Rich Classrooms With TeachGen@i", "title": "Prompting for Classroom Constraints", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting prompting for classroom constraints to TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "02", "section": "03", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i", "prompting_practice", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/02/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Prompting for Classroom Constraints\n\n![Prompting for Classroom Constraints textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/02-03-prompting-for-classroom-constraints.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nScenario setup tells the tool what room it is planning for; prompting for constraints tells it what it is not allowed to do. The two are different skills. A constraint is a boundary the plan must respect even when respecting it makes the plan worse on some other dimension: a hard time cap, a legally required accommodation, a no-homework week, a privacy rule. Generative tools are optimizers; left to their own devices they will quietly trade a constraint they were told to keep for an outcome they were trained to favor (more engagement, more coverage, a tidier flow). Prompting for constraints, and then verifying that the constraint actually held, is how a teacher keeps the tool inside the lines that law and equity draw.\n\nThis section teaches the difference between hard constraints (non-negotiable, often legal or ethical) and soft preferences (nice to have), and the discipline of checking compliance line by line rather than trusting that a stated constraint was honored. The transcript artifact records both the constraint you set and the verification you performed.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA middle-school teacher has a student whose IEP guarantees extended time on written responses, a hard 38-minute period, and a school directive: no homework this week. She prompts TeachGen@i for \"an engaging, rigorous civics lesson.\" The plan runs 55 minutes and ends with a take-home reading. She re-prompts, this time naming the time cap, the no-homework rule, and the extended-time accommodation. The new plan fits 38 minutes but drops the extended-time support to squeeze in a movement break. The tool treated a legal accommodation and a nice-to-have break as interchangeable constraints to juggle. The teacher's job is to mark which boundaries are immovable and verify they survived the optimization.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvery constraint you give a tool falls into one of two classes, and conflating them is the central error of constraint prompting.\n\n| | Hard constraint | Soft preference |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Definition | A boundary that must hold no matter the cost | A goal the plan should pursue if possible |\n| Typical source | Law, IEP/504, policy, ethics, the bell schedule | Pedagogical taste, pacing, variety |\n| Example | \"Extended response time for one student\" | \"Include a movement break\" |\n| If violated | The plan is unusable or unlawful | The plan is merely less ideal |\n| Correct instruction to the tool | \"Non-negotiable; never drop\" | \"Optional; drop this first if something must give\" |\n\nThe method has three moves. First, sort: label each constraint hard or soft before prompting, so you can tell the tool what it may sacrifice. Second, rank the soft preferences, so the tool knows the order to cut them in. Third, and most important, verify: read the returned plan against each hard constraint individually, because stating a constraint and the tool satisfying it are two separate events. TPACK keeps you honest about which content demands are real; critical edtech studies warns that an optimizer will always find a way to trade a quiet accommodation for a visible feature unless you forbid it explicitly and then check.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An IEP guarantees one student extended response time, and you also want a movement break. The tool can only fit one. Which is the hard constraint?\" explanation=\"A legally required IEP accommodation is a boundary that must hold no matter the cost; if it is dropped the plan is unusable and unlawful. The movement break is a soft preference: the plan is merely less ideal without it. Conflating the two is the central error of constraint prompting.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The extended response time, because an IEP accommodation is non-negotiable\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The movement break, because it keeps students engaged\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Both are equally hard, so the tool should decide\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWalk through one annotated judgment call on prompting for constraints. A middle-school teacher needs a 38-minute civics lesson, has a student with an IEP for extended response time, and may not assign homework this week.\n\n**Step 1. Sort hard from soft.** Hard constraints (must hold): 38-minute cap, extended-time accommodation, no homework. Soft preferences (nice to have): a short movement break, a current-events hook.\n\n**Step 2. Prompt and check, iteration by iteration.**\n\n> **Prompt 1:** \"Plan an engaging, rigorous civics lesson.\" Result ran 55 minutes and ended with a take-home reading. Annotated: adjectives are not constraints, and two hard limits were violated.\n\n> **Prompt 2:** \"Plan a 38-minute civics lesson, no homework, and build in extended response time for one student. A movement break is optional.\" Result dropped the extended-time support to fit a movement break. Annotated: the tool traded a hard accommodation for a soft preference.\n\n> **Prompt 3:** \"Protect extended response time as non-negotiable. Drop the movement break if needed. Keep the lesson at 38 minutes with no homework.\" Result honored all three hard constraints in 37 minutes.\n\n**Step 3. Verify, do not assume.** The teacher re-read prompt 3's plan line by line against each hard constraint and confirmed compliance, then logged the rejected movement-break suggestion with the reason. The transcript now shows that stating a constraint and satisfying it are two different events, and the teacher checked both.\n\nPredict what the optimizer does at each iteration before you reveal the result. The pattern is the lesson: an unguarded constraint gets traded away.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Watch the optimizer trade a constraint\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Prompt 1 is \\\"Plan an engaging, rigorous civics lesson\\\" with no constraints named. The hard limits are a 38-minute cap, extended response time for one student, and no homework. What is the optimizer most likely to do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Honor all three limits anyway\",\"Run long and assign homework because no limits were stated\",\"Ask you to clarify the constraints first\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The result ran 55 minutes and ended with a take-home reading. Adjectives are not constraints; two hard limits were violated because none were stated.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Prompt 2 now names all three hard limits but labels the movement break only as \u2019optional.\u2019 What does the optimizer do with the quiet accommodation?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Protects extended time and drops the optional break\",\"Drops the extended-time support to fit the movement break\",\"Keeps everything by adding minutes\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The plan dropped the extended-time support to squeeze in the movement break. The optimizer traded a hard accommodation for a soft preference because the accommodation was not marked non-negotiable.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Prompt 3 marks extended time as non-negotiable, tells the tool to drop the movement break first if needed, and re-states the 38-minute and no-homework limits. The plan honored all three hard constraints in 37 minutes, verified by a line-by-line read, with the rejected break logged.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nMark every hard constraint as non-negotiable, tell the tool which soft preference to cut first, and verify each hard constraint by reading the plan rather than trusting the prompt. A constraint you stated but did not check is a constraint you do not know you have.\n\nBefore accepting any plan, run the compliance pass:\n\n1. **Sort:** Is each constraint explicitly labeled hard or soft for the tool?\n2. **Rank:** Have you told the tool the order to drop soft preferences in?\n3. **Verify:** Have you read the plan against each hard constraint one at a time?\n4. **Log:** Did you record any hard constraint the tool violated and the re-prompt that fixed it?\n\nA plan that \"feels\" compliant has not been verified; only a line-by-line read counts.\n\nRun the sort-rank-verify discipline through a field-trip plan, where hard constraints multiply and the optimizer's temptation to favor a richer itinerary grows.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The field-trip compliance pass\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"You ask the tool for a museum field-trip plan. Hard constraints: a fixed 2:30 bus return, a district-mandated 1:8 chaperone ratio, a peanut-free lunch for one student, and a 200 dollars spending cap. The plan comes back rich and exciting. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Approve it; the platform badge says it meets safety standards\",\"next\":\"badge\",\"feedback\":\"A vendor compliance badge is a marketing surface, not a verified fit to your specific constraints. You have not checked anything yet.\"},{\"label\":\"Verify each hard constraint line by line before approving\",\"next\":\"verify\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Stating a constraint and the plan satisfying it are two separate events; only a read counts.\"}]},\"badge\":{\"prompt\":\"On the bus you discover only 3 chaperones for 32 students and a peanut-butter snack in the catered lunch. What does this teach?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A badge is not verification; check ratios and the allergy yourself\",\"next\":\"verify\",\"feedback\":\"Verification means counting chaperones against students and checking every food item, not trusting a claim.\"}]},\"verify\":{\"prompt\":\"Counting reveals the plan assigns 4 chaperones to 40 students (1:10) and the lunch vendor cannot confirm peanut-free. Which is the harder boundary to relax?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Both are hard; re-prompt to enforce 1:8 and a verified peanut-free vendor, drop a soft activity if budget forces it\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"You protected the legal ratio and the allergy as non-negotiable and let a soft preference give way to stay under 200 dollars.\"},{\"label\":\"Relax the ratio slightly to keep the fun extra activity\",\"next\":\"badge\",\"feedback\":\"The 1:8 ratio is district-mandated; relaxing it makes the plan unusable. A soft activity should give way first.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"Your transcript now shows the chaperone count, the verified peanut-free vendor, the cost total under 200 dollars, and the soft activity you cut, each with a reason.\",\"feedback\":\"This is constraint prompting as a verification habit: sort, rank, and verify each hard line by reading, not by trusting the prompt or the badge.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nThe first misreading is to assume that naming a constraint guarantees the tool kept it. Optimizers regress toward their training favorites; a stated accommodation can silently vanish in the next revision. The second is to over-trust the platform's own \"this plan meets accessibility standards\" badge and skip your own read. A vendor's compliance claim is a marketing surface, not a verified fit to your specific student's IEP. The under-documented constraint, the one you held in your head but never wrote into the prompt or the transcript, is the one most likely to be traded away and the hardest to defend later.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the sort-rank-verify method to a science lab. Hard constraints: a 50-minute period, a safety rule that the student who missed safety training does no open-flame work, and a budget line that allows no consumables over \\$40 this unit. Soft preferences, ranked: (1) a real-world phenomenon hook, (2) lab partners chosen by students, (3) a digital data-collection sheet. Prompt the tool with the hard constraints marked non-negotiable and the soft list in cut-order. When the plan returns, verify each hard line: search the plan for any open-flame step assigned to that student, total the consumable cost against \\$40, and confirm the timing. The transcript should show the cost arithmetic and the safety check explicitly, because \"I told it the budget\" is not evidence that the plan stayed under \\$40.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: in the left column list each constraint and whether it is hard or soft; in the right column write the verification result (held, violated, or fixed in re-prompt N). For the civics case, the left column says \"extended time, hard\" and the right column says \"violated in prompt 2, restored in prompt 3 after marking non-negotiable.\" A strong reader can look at any AI plan and ask, for each requirement, \"what is my evidence this held?\" If the only answer is \"I asked for it,\" the reading is not strong yet.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong constraint transcript labels each constraint hard or soft so a reviewer can see what was protected and what was tradeable. It shows at least one iteration where the tool violated a hard constraint and the re-prompt that forced compliance, because the violation-and-fix is the clearest evidence of verification. And it includes a concrete verification trace (the cost total, the line-by-line accommodation check), not just the assertion that the plan complied. Polish is a clean final prompt; evidence is the visible check that the boundaries held.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the method from a single lesson to a multi-day field trip plan. The hard constraints multiply and harden: a fixed bus return time, a 1:8 chaperone ratio mandated by district policy, a peanut-free lunch for one student, and a strict spending cap. The optimizer's temptation also grows, because a richer itinerary is easy to favor over a boring ratio rule. The sort-rank-verify discipline transfers exactly, but verification now means counting chaperones against students per group and checking every food vendor against the allergy, not just reading for time. If you can build the field-trip compliance pass without reusing the civics example, you have learned constraint prompting as a verification habit rather than a phrasing trick.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A colleague says, 'I told the tool the lesson must include extended response time, so the accommodation is covered.' In two or three sentences, explain why stating a constraint is not the same as the constraint holding, and describe the concrete verification you would perform on the returned plan.\" answer=\"An optimizer regresses toward its training favorites, so a stated accommodation can silently vanish in a later revision while the plan still looks complete. Stating a constraint and the tool satisfying it are two separate events. I would read the returned plan line by line for the specific student, confirming the extended-time support actually appears in the timing and directions, rather than trusting that asking for it guaranteed it.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/02-03-prompting-for-classroom-constraints-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"02.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions for CAT 531 02.03. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_prompting_for_classroom_constraints\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your transcript labels each constraint hard or soft, shows one hard-constraint violation and its fix, and records a concrete verification (a count, a cost total, a line-by-line read), not just the claim that the plan complied.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"prompting_practice\", \"cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "prompting_practice", "stem": "Which addition to a TeachGen@i prompt is an actual constraint the returned plan can be checked against?", "options": ["\"Make it really engaging and rigorous.\"", "\"Keep every direct-instruction segment to four minutes or less and assign no homework.\"", "\"Make sure students enjoy it.\"", "\"Use your best judgment to make it great.\""], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A constraint is checkable: a time cap and a no-homework rule can be verified in the plan. Adjectives like engaging and rigorous are wishes, not limits.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_more_detail_better", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_teaching_in_tech_rich_classrooms_with_teachgen_i", "prompting_practice", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "prompting_practice", "stem": "A prompt lists two requirements: \"a student with an IEP needs extended time\" and \"it would be nice to add a movement break.\" The AI returns a plan that drops extended time to fit the movement break. What is the right teacher move?", "options": ["Accept it, since the plan now includes the requested movement break.", "Re-prompt and protect the extended-time accommodation as non-negotiable, treating the movement break as optional.", "Drop both requirements to simplify the plan.", "Let students vote on which requirement to keep."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Extended time is a hard accommodation; a movement break is a soft preference. A defensible prompt never lets a preference override a legal or safety requirement.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_soft_vs_hard", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["prompting_practice", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "stem": "A teacher prompts \"no homework,\" the plan looks clean, and the teacher submits it. A colleague later finds a take-home reading buried in the closure. What does this miss?", "options": ["Nothing; stating the constraint in the prompt is enough.", "The teacher never re-read the returned plan against the no-homework constraint, so a stated constraint went unverified.", "The colleague is being too strict about homework.", "The plan should have been longer to be safe."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A stated constraint is not a satisfied constraint. After each prompt the teacher must re-read the plan against every hard limit.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_unverified_compliance", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["prompting_practice", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "Which transcript entry best documents disciplined prompting for constraints that a mentor could audit?", "options": ["A single polished prompt and a finished plan, with no iterations shown.", "Three prompt iterations: the first stated the 38-minute cap, the second re-prompted because the plan ran 52 minutes, and the third confirmed each segment was timed, with a note that an AI-suggested quiz was rejected for adding homework.", "A note saying the prompt worked on the first try.", "The number of words in the final prompt."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Disciplined prompting leaves an iteration trail: each constraint stated, checked against the returned plan, and re-prompted until satisfied, with rejections explained.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_unverified_compliance", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["prompting_practice", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_more_detail_better", "pattern": "verbosity_over_constraint", "description": "Believing a longer, more elaborate prompt is automatically a better-constrained prompt", "trigger": "teacher adds adjectives and wishes (engaging, rigorous, fun) but no measurable limits the plan must respect", "feedback": "A strong constraint is checkable: a time cap, a banned tool, a required standard, a reading level. Adjectives are not constraints.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_soft_vs_hard", "pattern": "soft_and_hard_constraints_treated_alike", "description": "Treating a preference (would be nice to include movement) the same as a non-negotiable limit (IEP requires extended time)", "trigger": "teacher lets the AI trade away a legal or safety requirement to satisfy a stylistic wish", "feedback": "Separate hard constraints the plan must satisfy from soft preferences. Never let a soft preference override an accommodation or safety rule.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_2_3_prompting_for_classroom_constraints_unverified_compliance", "pattern": "assumed_constraint_compliance", "description": "Assuming the AI honored a stated constraint without checking the returned plan against it", "trigger": "teacher states no homework but the plan still assigns reading at home, and the teacher submits it unchecked", "feedback": "After each prompt, re-read the plan against every hard constraint. A stated constraint is not a satisfied constraint.", "rail_action": "explain"}]}